//Brute Force – Generate All Pairs + Sorting
**Complexity:**

There are n² possible pairs.

- Generating pairs: O(n²)
- Sorting all sums: O(n² log(n²))
- Extracting k elements: O(k)

**Overall Time Complexity:**

O(n² log n)

**Space Complexity:**

O(n²)

**Problem:**

We generate all possible combinations, even though we only need the k largest sums. This results in unnecessary computations and extra memory usage.


class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> topKSumPairs(vector<int>& a, vector<int>& b, int k) {

        int n = a.size();

        vector<int> sums;

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                sums.push_back(a[i] + b[j]);
            }
        }

        sort(sums.rbegin(), sums.rend());

        vector<int> ans;

        for(int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            ans.push_back(sums[i]);
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

//better : Better – Max Heap + Generate All Pairs
**Complexity:**

- Generate and insert n² sums: O(n² log(n²))
- Extract k maximum sums: O(k log(n²))

**Overall Time Complexity:**

O(n² log n + k log n)

**Space Complexity:**

O(n²)

**Difference from Brute Force:**

We avoid explicitly sorting all the sums, but still store all n² combinations in the priority queue.

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> topKSumPairs(vector<int>& a, vector<int>& b, int k) {

        int n = a.size();

        priority_queue<int> pq;

        for(int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                pq.push(a[i] + b[j]);
            }
        }

        vector<int> ans;

        while(k--) {
            ans.push_back(pq.top());
            pq.pop();
        }

        return ans;
    }
};



//optimal
Instead of generating all \(n^2\) combinations, we can start with the largest possible combination and explore the next largest combinations.

First, sort both arrays in descending order.
### **Complexity Analysis**

Let n be the size of each array.

| Operation                     | Complexity |
| ----------------------------- | ---------- |
| Sort both arrays              | O(n log n) |
| Initial heap insertion        | O(log k)   |
| Extract k elements            | O(k log k) |
| Generate at most 2k neighbors | O(k log k) |
| Visited set operations        | O(k log k) |

**Overall Time Complexity:**

O(n log n + k log k)

Since the problem constraints specify k ≤ n:

O(n log n)

**Space Complexity:**

- Max Heap: O(k)
- Visited Set: O(k)

**Total Space Complexity:**

O(k)

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> topKSumPairs(vector<int>& a, vector<int>& b, int k) {

        int n = a.size();

        sort(a.rbegin(), a.rend());
        sort(b.rbegin(), b.rend());

        priority_queue<pair<int, pair<int,int>>> pq;

        set<pair<int,int>> visited;

        pq.push({a[0] + b[0], {0, 0}});
        visited.insert({0, 0});

        vector<int> ans;

        while(k-- && !pq.empty()) {

            auto p = pq.top();
            pq.pop();

            int sum = p.first;
            int i = p.second.first;
            int j = p.second.second;

            ans.push_back(sum);

            // Next element from array a
            if(i + 1 < n && !visited.count({i + 1, j})) {

                pq.push({
                    a[i + 1] + b[j],
                    {i + 1, j}
                });

                visited.insert({i + 1, j});
            }

            // Next element from array b
            if(j + 1 < n && !visited.count({i, j + 1})) {

                pq.push({
                    a[i] + b[j + 1],
                    {i, j + 1}
                });

                visited.insert({i, j + 1});
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};


